	(build-host)$ bitbake xilinx-bootbin

The BOOT.BIN is located in the following directory:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-versal/boot.bin

The generated BOOT.BIN can be installed to either SD Card or QSPI. You can
refer to the sections "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode" and "4.2.2 QSPI Boot Mode" for
the detailed steps.

Alternatively, you can generate a WIC image with the BOOT.BIN installed by
building wrlinux-image-std instead of xilinx-bootbin:

	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

Then, you can refer to the section "5. WIC Notes" to flash this WIC image
to SD card.

Note: Check if you set WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME correctly in conf/local.conf
when encountering the following build error:
	File "layers/oe-core/bitbake/lib/bb/parse/parse_py/ConfHandler.py", line 97, in include_single_file
	raise ParseError("Could not %s file %s" % (error_out, fn), parentfn, lineno)
	bb.parse.ParseError: ParseError at layers/amd-versal/conf/machine/amd-versal-sdt.inc:5: Could not include required file conf/machine/<workflow_machine_name>.conf

7.1.4.6 Rebuild
---------------
If you want to run gen-machineconf to create a new <workflow_machine_name>
based on an existing project, you need to clean the build files by running
the following commands:

	(build-host)$ rm conf/machine -Rf; rm output -Rf; rm conf/dts -Rf; rm hw-description -Rf
	(build-host)$ sed -i '/WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME/d' conf/local.conf

After re-running the gen-machineconf command, do not forget to reconfigure
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME in conf/local.conf.

7.2 Device Tree Overlay
-----------------------
The Device Tree Overlay(DTO) is used to add the child nodes and the fragments
from the .dtbo file to the base device tree, then triggering drivers to be
probed after PL programming. So the dtbo file for PL needs to be installed
to rootfs along with PL firmwares (including Bitstream or PDI).

The recipe "versal-fw-demo" in the amd-versal BSP layer is designed to
generate the dtbo file in WRLinux by utilizing the bbclass "dfx_user_dts".
For a more detailed description of "dfx_user_dts", please see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/meta-xilinx/blob/rel-v2025.2/docs/README.dfx.user.dts.md

After running the steps in the section "7.1.4 SDT Flow", execute the
following commands:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cp conf/dts/<your_workflow_machine_name>/pl-overlay-full/pl.dtsi <PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-versal/recipes-firmware/versal-fw-demo/files
	(build-host)$ cp hw-description/<path_to_pld>/<proj>_pld.pdi <PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-versal/recipes-firmware/versal-fw-demo/files
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " versal-fw-demo"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:wr-amd-versal += 'versal-fw-demo'
FW_DEMO_FILES:pn-versal-fw-demo = "pl.dtsi <proj>_pld.pdi"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

The dtbo and pdi files can be found at the boards:
	root@amd-versal:~# ls -l /lib/firmware/xilinx/versal-fw-demo/versal-fw-demo.*
	-rw-r--r-- 1 root root    3825 Apr  5  2011 /lib/firmware/xilinx/versal-fw-demo/versal-fw-demo.dtbo
	-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 1423280 Apr  5  2011 /lib/firmware/xilinx/versal-fw-demo/versal-fw-demo.pdi

These files can be used to program PL by following the instructions in
the section "7.3 PL Programming".

7.3 PL Programming
------------------
There are 3 methods to program PL from the Linux side:
    - sysfs
    - configfs
    - fpgautil

For a more detailed description of PL programming, please see
	https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/1188397412/Solution+Versal+PL+Programming

7.3.1 Sysfs
-----------
Run the following commands to program PL via sysfs:

	root@amd-versal:~# echo 0 > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/flags
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir -p /lib/firmware
	root@amd-versal:~# cp <path_to_pdi/proj_pld.pdi> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# echo proj_pld.pdi > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/firmware

Note: The sysfs method only supports the PL programming without dtbo.

7.3.2 Configfs
--------------
Run the following commands to program PL via configfs:

	root@amd-versal:~# echo 0 > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/flags
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir -p /lib/firmware
	root@amd-versal:~# cp <path_to_pdi/proj_pld.pdi> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# cp <path_to_dtbo/pl.dtbo> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system
	root@amd-versal:~# cd /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# echo pl.dtbo > /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system/path

Steps to remove the drivers that were added as part of DTO

	root@amd-versal:~# rmdir /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system

7.3.3 Fpgautil
--------------
The fpgautil tool provides a user-friendly interface for loading,
reconfiguring and reading FPGA bitstream configurations. Follow
the steps to install this tool on WRLinux:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fpga-manager-script'
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " fpga-manager-script"
_EOF

Run the following commands to program PL via fpgautil:
    root@amd-versal:~# fpgautil -b <path_to_pdi/proj_pld.pdi> -o <path_to_dtbo/pl.dtbo>

7.4 XRT
-------
Xilinx Runtime (XRT) is an open-source software stack designed to manage
and utilize FPGAs and AI Engines (AIE) for accelerating compute-intensive
applications. It provides a standardized software interface, including
APIs and drivers, that acts as a low-level communication layer between
Linux apps and the Versal boards.

It consists of the following components:
	- XRT Runtime Libraries
	- ZOCL Driver
	- Tools (xclbinutil, etc.)

For a more detailed description of XRT, please see
	https://xilinx.github.io/XRT/2025.2/html/index.html

7.4.1 Building and Installation
-------------------------------
The building and installation of XRT are based on any one of the above
sections: "7.1.3 Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp" or "7.1.4 SDT Flow". In other
words, after completing the steps in any of the two sections mentioned
above, proceed with the next steps.

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:openembedded-layer += 'ocl-icd'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:openembedded-layer += 'rapidjson'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'ai-engine-driver'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'aiefal'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'libdfx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xrt'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'zocl'
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xrt"
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " xrt zocl"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

7.4.2 Usage
-----------
The usage of the XRT tools will be mentioned in the section "7.6 AI Engine".

7.5 WRLinux Common Image
------------------------
AMD provides a package named "xilinx-versal-common-v2025.2.tar.gz", which
contains a prebuilt Linux kernel and root file system that can be used
with the Versal board for embedded Vitis platform developers. It is also
required in the AI development process.

WRLinux provides a mechanism to generate a similar package. Using this
mechanism, customers can customize their own common image package.

7.5.1 Building
--------------
The building of WRLinux Common Image is based on any one of above sections
"7.1.3 Meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp" or "7.1.4 SDT Flow". In other
words, after completing the steps in any of the two sections mentioned
above, proceed with the next steps.

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:wr-amd-versal += 'wrlinux-versal-common'
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-versal-common

After the bitbake command completes, the package will be found at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-versal/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2.tar.gz

7.5.2 Installation
------------------
Execute the following steps to install the WRLinux Common Image to
the host-pc with Vitis/Vivado installed.

	(build-host)$ tar xvf wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2.tar.gz -C <your_sdk_path>

The following steps are to install the SDK:

	(build-host)$ sh <your_sdk_path>/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2/sdk.sh
	Wind River Linux LTS SDK installer version 25.33
	================================================
	Enter target directory for SDK (default: /opt/windriver/wrlinux/25.33):<your_sdk_path>/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2
	You are about to install the SDK to "<your_sdk_path>/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2". Proceed [Y/n]? Y
	Extracting SDK.......................done
	Setting it up...done
	SDK has been successfully set up and is ready to be used.
	Each time you wish to use the SDK in a new shell session, you need to source the environment setup script e.g.
	$ . <your_sdk_path>/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2/environment-setup-cortexa72-cortexa53-wrs-linux

Input the install path and press "Y" as shown in the demonstration above.
Then check if all the files are correctly installed:

	(build-host)$ ls <your_sdk_path>/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2/
	bl31.elf	Image	rootfs.tar.gz	sysroots boot.scr	rootfs.ext4	sdk.sh	u-boot.elf
	environment-setup-cortexa72-cortexa53-wrs-linux  rootfs.manifest	site-config-cortexa72-cortexa53-wrs-linux
	version-cortexa72-cortexa53-wrs-linux

7.6 AI Engine
-------------
Note: The AI Engine is available on the VCK190 board only.

7.6.1 Using WRLinux Common Image
--------------------------------
This flow uses the WRLinux Common Image to help generate the final SD card
image and the applications that run on PS. The following is a simplified
flowchart for this flow.

                           +----------------+  +---------------+  +---------------------+
                           | PS application |  |    FPGA       |  | AI Engine Component |
                           |                |  |               |  |                     |
                           |    main.cpp    |  | HLS Component |  |  kern.cc adf.cxx    |
                           +----------------+  +---------------+  +---------------------+
                                   |                   |                    |
  +--------------------+           |                   |                    |
  |WRLinux Common Image|-----  SYSROOT (XRT)     v++ --mode hls        v++ --mode aie
  +--------------------+         and GCC               |                    |
                 |                 |               +---------+          +---------+
                 |            +---------+          |kernel.xo|          |libadf.a |
          +-------------+     |host.elf |          +---------+          +---------+
          |boot.scr     |     +---------+               |                   |
          |rootfs.tar.gz|          |                    |-------------------|
          |Image        |          |                               |
          |u-boot.elf   |          |                               |
          |rootfs.ext4  |          |                            v++ --link
          |bl31.elf     |          |                               |
          +-------------+          |                               |
                 |                 |                          +---------+
                 |------------ v++ --package  --------------- |a.xclbin |
                                   |                          +---------+
                                   |
                             +-------------+
                             | sd_card.img |
                             +-------------+

To achieve the above process to utilize AI Engine, the following components
are required:

	- ZOCL: A GEM-style out-of-tree kernel driver (In rootfs)
	- XRT: XRT Runtime Libraries and Management Tools (In rootfs)
	- AIE: AI Engine kernel drivers (In Image)

We need to generate and install a WRLinux common image by following the steps
in "7.4 XRT" and "7.5 WRLinux Common Image".

Next, we will use an example in Vitis-Tutorials to illustrate how to use the
AI Engine.

	(build-host)$ git clone -b 2025.2 https://github.com/Xilinx/Vitis-Tutorials.git
	(build-host)$ cd Vitis-Tutorials/AI_Engine_Development/AIE/Design_Tutorials/01-aie_lenet_tutorial
	(build-host)$ sed -i '/export COMMON_IMAGE_VERSAL/c\export COMMON_IMAGE_VERSAL=<your_sdk_path>/wrlinux-versal-common-v2025.2/' sample_env_setup.sh
	(build-host)$ sed -i 's/environment-setup-cortexa72-cortexa53-amd-linux/environment-setup-cortexa72-cortexa53-wrs-linux/g' sample_env_setup.sh

Additionally, it is necessary to configure the environment variables
XILINX_VITIS and PLATFORM_REPO_PATHS correctly in sample_env_setup.sh
based on the installation of Vitis in your host-pc.

	(build-host)$ source sample_env_setup.sh
	(build-host)$ make TARGET=hw all

After the above commands complete successfully, an image "sd_card.img" is
generated and we need to burn it to SD card at host-pc:

	(build-host)$ sudo dd if=./build/lenet_x1/hw/package/sd_card.img of=/dev/sdaX

Insert the SD card to the VCK190 board, power on the board, and wait for it
to finish booting up the WRLinux, then log in the console.

On the login console, execute the following commands to check if the ZOCL
driver is correctly loaded:

	root@amd-versal:~# ls -l /dev/dri/by-path/*
	lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root  8 Nov 13 02:12 /dev/dri/by-path/platform-amba_pl:zyxclmm_drm-card -> ../card0
	lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 13 Nov 13 02:12 /dev/dri/by-path/platform-amba_pl:zyxclmm_drm-render -> ../renderD128

Check the status of XRT libraries and Device:

	root@amd-versal:~# xrt-smi examine
	......
	System Configuration
	  OS Name              : Linux
	  Release              : 6.12.64-yocto-standard
	  Machine              : aarch64
	  CPU Cores            : 2
	  Memory               : 1692 MB
	  Distribution         : Wind River Linux LTS 25.33 Update 5
	  GLIBC                : 2.41
	  Model                : Xilinx Versal vck190 Eval board rev1.1
	  BIOS Vendor          : unknown
	  BIOS Version         : unknown

	XRT
	  Version              : 2.20.0
	  Branch               : 2025.2
	  Hash                 : 74dec3c9c4f6dd9bfb62ae11e71ec96bcc4859eb
	  Hash Date            : 2025-11-11 22:38:22
	  zocl                 : 2.20.0, a9e65f10c3f0cac0f915060774a339ab7ecd5a97
	  Firmware Version     : N/A

	Device(s) Present
	|BDF             |Shell  |Logic UUID  |Device ID     |Device Ready*  |
	|----------------|-------|------------|--------------|---------------|
	|[0000:00:00.0]  |edge   |0x0         |user(inst=0)  |Yes            |

Run the "lenet_aie_xrt" demo:

	root@amd-versal:~# mount /dev/mmcblk0p1 /mnt
	root@amd-versal:~# cd /mnt
	root@amd-versal:~# ./lenet_aie_xrt.elf a.xclbin
	Initializing ADF API...
	Iteration : 1...
	zocl-drm amba_pl:zyxclmm_drm: zocl_create_client: created KDS client for pid(559), ret: 0
	zocl-drm amba_pl:zyxclmm_drm: zocl_destroy_client: client exits pid(559)
	zocl-drm amba_pl:zyxclmm_drm: zocl_create_client: created KDS client for pid(559), ret: 0
	[drm] Memory 0 is not reserved in device tree. Will allocate memory from CMA
	[drm] Memory 3 is not reserved in device tree. Will allocate memory from CMA
	[drm] AIE create successfully finished.
	......
	Pass for image 0
	Pass for image 1
	......
	Pass for image 99
	Releasing remaining XRT objects...
	graph end
	TEST PASSED

To report xclbin information:
	root@amd-versal:~# xclbinutil --info --input a.xclbin

To extract the bitstream image:
	root@amd-versal:~# xclbinutil --dump-section BITSTREAM:RAW:bitstream.bit --input a.xclbin

To extract the build metadata
	root@amd-versal:~# xclbinutil --dump-section BUILD_METADATA:HTML:buildMetadata.json --input a.xclbin

8. Known Issues
===============

8.1 GEM Suspend
---------------
The GEM MAC suspend/resume is not supported.
